.TH GIT-DONE 1 "2026-07-07" "gitplus" "gitplus Manual"
.SH NAME
git-done \- switch back to the base branch and delete the branch you left
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B git done
[\fB\-f\fR|\fB\-\-force\fR]
[\fB\-b\fR|\fB\-\-base\fR \fINAME\fR]
[\fB\-n\fR|\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR]
[\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The bookend to
.BR git-pr (1).
After you have checked out a pull request's branch and finished reviewing it,
.B git done
returns you to the base branch (origin's default, usually
.IR main ),
fast-forwards it to the latest on
.IR origin ,
and deletes the branch you were on.
.PP
The fast-forward is best effort: it uses
.B git merge \-\-ff\-only
so it never creates a merge commit, and it is skipped with a note if you are
offline or the local base has diverged from
.IR origin .
Refreshing the base first also means the merged-check below is judged against an
up-to-date base.
.PP
Deleting is safe by default: the branch you left is only deleted if it is
actually merged into the base, checked explicitly with
.BR "git merge-base --is-ancestor" ,
.B not
a bare
.BR "git branch \-d" .
That distinction matters:
.B \-d
checks mergedness against a branch's own upstream when it has one, not
against the base you are switching to \(em so a branch that has simply been
.B push
ed (true of anything that has ever had a pull request) passes
.BR \-d 's
check regardless of whether it was ever merged into the base, silently
dropping real work.
.PP
Squash- and rebase-merged branches are handled as well, and have to be:
neither is an ancestor of the base (a squash is one new commit; a rebase
rewrites them), so the ancestor test alone would refuse every branch forever
in a repository that squash-merges \(em the command never doing its job. When
that test says no, GitHub is asked whether a pull request from this branch
into
.B this
base was merged. The base match is the point: a stacked PR merged into its
.B parent
branch has not reached the base, and is still correctly refused. Requires
.BR gh ;
offline it falls back to refusing, which is the safe direction.
.B \-\-force
still deletes regardless.
.PP
On a detached HEAD there is no branch to delete, so
.B git done
just switches back to the base (and fast-forwards it), then reports that there
was nothing to delete.
.PP
If you are already on the base branch, there is likewise nothing to delete, so
.B git done
simply pulls it \(em fetches and fast-forwards the base to
.IR origin .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-force
Force-delete the branch with
.BR "git branch \-D" ,
deleting it even when it is not fully merged. Use for squash-merged or otherwise
finished branches that the default
.B \-d
refuses.
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-base " " \fINAME\fR
Use
.I NAME
as the base branch instead of origin's default.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Print what would happen \(em the switch, fast-forward, and delete \(em without
changing anything.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show usage and exit.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B git pr 923 && git done
Check out PR #923, review it, then return to main and delete its branch.
.TP
.B git done \-f
Force-delete the current branch (e.g. after it was squash-merged).
.TP
.B git done \-n
Preview the actions without running them.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Exits nonzero if not in a git repository, the base is not found, or the branch
is refused by a safe delete.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR git-pr (1),
.BR git-sync (1),
.BR git-sweep (1),
.BR git-branch (1),
.BR git-switch (1)
